@synopsis
**yume-setup** **init** **--host** *host* **--output** *directory* [ **--port** *port* ] [ **--client-name** *name* ] [ **--max-sessions** *count* ] [ **--weight** *weight* ] [ **--max-egress-mbps** *rate* ] [ **--preset** *preset* ]
**yume-setup** **add-client** **--server** *directory* **--host** *host* **--output** *directory* **--client-name** *name* [ **--max-sessions** *count* ] [ **--weight** *weight* ] [ **--circuits** ]
**yume-setup** **remove-client** **--server** *directory* **--client-name** *name*
**yume-setup** **cluster-init** **--output** *directory*
**yume-setup** **cluster-add** **--cluster** *directory* **--server** *directory* **--name** *name* **--host** *host* [ **--address** *address* ] [ **--exit** ]
**yume-setup** **cluster-remove** **--cluster** *directory* **--name** *name*
**yume-setup** **cluster-sign** **--cluster** *directory* [ **--days** *days* ]
**yume-setup** **--help**
@end

## DESCRIPTION

**yume-setup** writes the files man:yumed(8) and man:yume(1) run from: a
server directory with its configuration, credentials and cover site, client
bundles for it, and an operator's signed cluster list. It starts neither
program and opens no network connection, except that **cluster-sign**
resolves a node's host when the node has no address.

Keys, certificates and signatures come from the OpenSSL release YUME is
built with, through the same readers **yume** and **yumed** use, so setup
needs no **openssl** command or other runtime. Every key is read back by
those readers before setup writes it.

Secret files are created owner-only, exclusively and without following a
symlink. A new kit, bundle or operator directory is written under a private
staging name beside its target and renamed into place without replacing
anything, so a failed command leaves no partial directory. Commands that
change an existing server or cluster write each replaced file beside it and
rename it over the old one. Each replacement is atomic, but a command that
touches several files is not one filesystem transaction.

## ACTIONS

@options
@opt **init**

Create a new kit directory holding **server/** and a first client bundle in
**client/**. The server gets a composite Ed25519 and ML-DSA-87 identity, an
ML-KEM-1024 key, a P-256 TLS certificate from a one-off CA whose key is never
written, the deployment's admission key, an authorized-keys store with the
client, an empty admin-keys store and a static cover site. The client gets
its own identity, its access PSK and the server's public material.
@opt **add-client**

Issue another client bundle for an existing server directory. The client is
added to the server's authorized-keys store with the kit's **tcp** and
**udp** services, and the store is replaced last. Nothing changes unless
every step succeeds. Files added to the server tree keep the store's owner.
A running daemon accepts the client once it reloads (SIGHUP).
@opt **remove-client**

Take a client out of a server's authorized-keys store, then delete its
public key and access PSK. The last client cannot be removed, because the
daemon requires one. A running daemon ends the client's sessions when it
reloads.
@opt **cluster-init**

Create an operator directory with a new composite operator key, the key
network tags are made under and an empty cluster record. Keep it off the
servers.
@opt **cluster-add**

Enter a server directory into a cluster. The server's TLS certificate must
name **--host**. For every node already in the cluster, a fresh PSK for each
direction goes into both nodes' peer stores with the other node's admission
key, and the new server gets the operator's public key and a **cluster**
section in its **yumed.json**.
@opt **cluster-remove**

Take a node out of a cluster. Every other node forgets it, and the removed
node loses its cluster section, its **credentials/cluster** directory and the
serial it saved, so it runs alone after a restart. Sign a new list
afterwards.
@opt **cluster-sign**

Sign the next cluster list and routes view and copy them to every node. The
serial goes up by one, and both expire after **--days**. The routes view
names each node, its identity, its exit mark and its network tag, and no
address. Both are checked with the readers the nodes use before anything is
written.
@end

## OPTIONS

@options
@opt **--host** *host*
@cli spell: --host <host>
@cli help: The server's public DNS name or IP address

The DNS name or IP literal the server's TLS certificate names, which clients
authenticate. Required by **init**, **add-client** and **cluster-add**.
@opt **--output** *directory*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --output <directory>
@cli help: New directory to create; its parent must exist

The new directory **init**, **add-client** or **cluster-init** writes. Its
parent must be an existing directory, and nothing may exist at the path.
@opt **--port** *port*
@cli spell: --port <port>
@cli help: The server's port, 443 by default

The port **init** writes into both configurations, from 1 to 65535.
@opt **--client-name** *name*
@cli spell: --client-name <name>
@cli help: The client's name in the store, client1 by default

The client's name in the authorized-keys store: 1 to 63 letters, digits,
**.**, **_** or **-**, beginning and ending with a letter or digit.
**init** uses **client1** without it, and **add-client** and
**remove-client** require it.
@opt **--max-sessions** *count*
@cli spell: --max-sessions <count>
@cli help: Sessions the client may hold at once, 1 to 1024

How many sessions the client may hold at once, from 1 to 1024. A newer
session replaces the oldest. Without it the daemon sets no per-client bound.
@opt **--weight** *weight*
@cli spell: --weight <weight>
@cli help: The client's share of the egress rate, 0.1 to 100

The client's share of the server's egress rate against other busy clients,
from 0.1 to 100.
@opt **--max-egress-mbps** *rate*
@cli spell: --max-egress-mbps <rate>
@cli help: The rate clients share by weight, in Mbit/s

For **init**: the server's stream egress rate in Mbit/s, from 1 to
1000000, which clients share by weight. Unlimited when omitted.
@opt **--preset** *preset*
@cli values: stealth balanced fast max
@cli spell: --preset <preset>
@cli help: Tuning for both sides: stealth (default), balanced,
@cli help: fast or max

For **init**: the tuning preset both configurations take, which trades
far-path speed against how much data one key protects and how close the
carrier stays to browser traffic. **stealth** is the default. A client from
**add-client** takes its server's tuning.
@opt **--server** *directory*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --server <directory>
@cli help: A server directory holding yumed.json

A server directory in the layout **init** writes, holding **yumed.json** and
its credentials.
@opt **--circuits**
@cli spell: --circuits
@cli help: Send the client's connections through circuits

For **add-client**: grant the client **yume.circuit** and give its bundle a
**circuits** section of three hops, with this server as the entry, and the
kit's copies of the operator key and routes view. The server must belong to
a signed cluster. Circuits carry TCP only, so the bundle's SOCKS5 adapter
refuses UDP.
@opt **--cluster** *directory*
@cli file: yes
@cli spell: --cluster <directory>
@cli help: The operator directory from cluster-init

The operator directory **cluster-init** created, which holds the operator
key and the cluster record. Required by every cluster action.
@opt **--name** *name*
@cli spell: --name <name>
@cli help: The node's name in the cluster list

The node's name in the cluster list, by the same rule as a client name.
@opt **--address** *address*
@cli spell: --address <address>
@cli help: An IP address other nodes dial instead of the host

For **cluster-add**: an IP literal other nodes dial instead of resolving
**--host**. TLS still authenticates the host. It also gives the node's
network tag; without it **cluster-sign** resolves the host.
@opt **--exit**
@cli spell: --exit
@cli help: Carry circuits' streams through the node's direct_tcp

For **cluster-add**: the node carries circuits' streams to destinations
through its one **direct_tcp** adapter and that adapter's destination policy.
@opt **--days** *days*
@cli spell: --days <days>
@cli help: Days the list stays valid, 1 to 366 (default 30)

For **cluster-sign**: how long the list and routes view stay valid, from 1 to
366 days. A node whose list has expired closes its links.
@opt **-h, --help**
@cli spell: -h, --help
@cli help: Print this help and exit

Print the actions and options and exit.
@end

An option may also be written as **--option=value**.

## EXIT STATUS

**0** on success, **1** when the action fails, and **2** for a usage error.
A failure prints one line beginning **yume-setup:** and names no secret.

## FILES

A kit from **init** holds **manifest.json**, **server/yumed.json** with
**server/credentials/**, **server/cover-site/**, **server/services/** and
**server/start-server**, and **client/yume.json** with
**client/credentials/**, **client/adapters/** and **client/start-client**.
**docs/OPERATIONS.md** describes deploying them and
**docs/protocol/CLUSTER_1.md** the cluster files.

## EXAMPLES

@code bash
yume-setup init --host vpn.example.net --output "$PWD/yume-kit"
yume-setup add-client --server yume-kit/server --host vpn.example.net \
  --output "$PWD/tablet" --client-name tablet --max-sessions 2
yume-setup remove-client --server yume-kit/server --client-name tablet
@end

## SEE ALSO

